<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Franz Liszt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Franz Liszt was a Hungarian composer, pianist and teacher, the most celebrated piano virtuoso of the nineteenth century. After years of touring, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a> from 1848 to 1861, where he conducted works by Wagner and Berlioz and created the symphonic poem. His Hungarian Rhapsodies, piano sonata and many transcriptions remain central to the repertoire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/franz-liszt.jpg" alt="Franz Liszt" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herman Biow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Liszt_by_Herman_Biow-_1843.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
